Q: &quot;key&quot; prop. Check the render method of `div` A: <p>Corrected code<br />
Here, I have added the array index as the <code>key</code>. However, if you have any <strong>unique and stable value</strong> in the array, you should assign that value to the <code>key</code> instead.</p>
<pre><code>{headerData.map((item, index) =&gt; (
                        &lt;button key={index} className=&quot;flex items-center gap-1.5 font-semibold shrink-0 hover:text-blue-600 transition-colors&quot;&gt;
                            ....
                        &lt;/button&gt;
                    ))}
</code></pre>
<p>To avoid errors, you must pass the <code>key</code> to the button, not to a child of the button. Also, you are using a button and a <code>Link</code> together, which can cause errors. Use either a button or a <code>Link</code> at a time—do not use both together. Keys should be unique and stable</p>
<p><strong>Note:</strong> The <code>key</code> should always be placed on the first (top-level) element of the iteration or loop.</p>

Q: Using enable_if on virtual functions A: <p>You can solve this by specializing the class template with <code>enable_if</code> instead of using <code>enable_if</code> on the method:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;type_traits&gt;

class Base {
public:
    virtual bool f() = 0;
};

template&lt;typename T, typename enable = void&gt; class Derived;

template&lt;typename T&gt; class Derived&lt;T, std::enable_if_t&lt;std::is_copy_constructible&lt;T&gt;::value&gt;&gt;: public Base {
public:
    bool f() override {
        return true;
    }
};

template&lt;typename T&gt; class Derived&lt;T, std::enable_if_t&lt;!std::is_copy_constructible&lt;T&gt;::value&gt;&gt;: public Base {
public:
    bool f() override {
        return false;
    }
};
</code></pre>
